<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.14.2: Finding a Pair of Values that Satisfies Multiple Inequalities</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00628</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.14.2: Finding a Pair of Values that Satisfies Multiple Inequalities</md:title>
    <md:uuid>407f44dc-bd8e-4571-942b-8803737d5757</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To make a quilt, a quilter is buying fabric in two colors, light and dark. He needs at least 9.5 yards of fabric in total.</para>
<para>The light color costs $9 a yard. The dark color costs $13 a yard. The quilter can spend up to $110 on fabric.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Close up of a quilt"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/493fb288fa204a57b2b8410edc19f6744c48edd1"/></media></figure></para>
<newline/><para>Here are two graphs that represent the two constraints.</para>

<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
  <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
    <para>Length</para>
    <figure><media alt="Inequality graphed on a coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a56e66d40ef25b3ec1a6968f8b8681a27ec30bfa"/></media></figure></div>
  <div class="os-raise-mx-auto">
    <para>Cost</para>
    <figure><media alt="Inequality graphed on a coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/682cd7e478132429eba81c5915080a23bf10de77"/></media></figure></div>
</div>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an inequality to represent the length constraint. Let \(x\) represent the yards of light fabric and \(y\) represent the yards of dark fabric.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: You answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\(x+y\geq9.5\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="53488a62-2b6e-4403-a16e-d01348f09e52"><problem><para>
      <para>Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> pairs that satisfy the length constraint.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>["\\((5,5)\\)","\\((7.5,3.5)\\)" ,"\\((12,10)\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answers are \((5,5)\), \((7.5,3.5)\), and \((12,10)\).</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--END MULTISELECT -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an inequality to represent the cost constraint.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\(9x+13y\leq110\)  </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="30eb6fbd-3d3e-4d80-b648-6d59163b5920"><problem><para>
      <para>Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> the pairs that satisfy the cost constraint.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>["\\((1,1)\\)","\\((4,5)\\)" ,"\\((10,1)\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>Nice try! The correct answers are \((1,1)\), \((4,5)\), and \((10,1)\).</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><!--END MULTISELECT -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain why \((2,2)\) satisfies the cost constraint but not the length constraint.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>2 yards of each color costs $44, which is less than $110, so it satisfies this constraint. However, this is only 4 yards altogether, which is not at least 9.5 yards.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find at least one pair of numbers that satisfies both constraints. Be prepared to explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\((10,1)\), \((8,2)\), and (\(6.5,3.5)\) (any point that is in the intersection of the two inequalities).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does the pair of numbers represent in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Yoru answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\((10,1)\) means 10 yards of light fabric and 1 yard of dark fabric. This is 11 yards of fabric altogether, which is more than 9.5. It costs $103 because \(9(10)+1(13)=103\). This is less than the $110 maximum.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the two inequalities together as a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">system of inequalities</term>.</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}9x+13y\leq110\\x+y\geq9.5\end{array}\right.\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
